For consistency across 8-10 pages while keeping things self-contained for a beginner site, embedded internal styles work great. Place your basic CSS inside a single `<style>` block in the `<head>` of a shared master template (or each page directly if you're keeping it even simpler for testing). That way you can copy-paste the same styles across files easily without worrying about external linking or caching issues—it stays fully self-contained and changes show up instantly in the browser.

Tips for a responsive nav bar: Use a simple unordered list inside a container div, add basic flex or grid layout for desktop alignment, then switch to a hamburger-style stack on smaller screens using CSS media queries with min-width breakpoints. Keep anchor tags for links so everything stays pure HTML/JS.

Here's a complete self-contained starting example you can copy into your `index.html` (or similar for hobby.html and work.html):

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <style>
        body { font-family: sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 20px; }
        nav { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; background: #f8f9fa; padding: 1em; }
        nav ul { display: flex; gap: 20px; }
        nav ul li { position: relative; }
        nav ul li:hover::after { content: 'v'; position: absolute; left: 0; bottom: -5px; }
        nav ul li a { text-decoration: none; color: #333; }
        nav ul li.active a { font-weight: bold; text-shadow: 0 0 2px black; }
        nav ul li a:hover { color: #007bff; }
        @media (max-width: 600px) {
            nav ul { display: none; flex-direction: column; position: absolute; top: 60px; left: 0; right: 0; background: white; }
        }
    </style>
</head>
